  "notes": "⛔ **`low`, and the research says why: nothing describes his programming directly.** What is sourced is a real name (Dwan Avery), a city, 808 Mafia membership, an imprint, a nine-track verified credit board across four charting albums, two key readings and a tempo band — and that is what this model is built from. `session.keys` is the strongest field here: A♭ and F **major** on the verified readings, with a minor-coloured ostinato laid over the top rather than a progression, which is why major and mixolydian outweigh the minor scales and `halfStepDissonance` sits at 0.2 over a major tonic. `bpm` 144–152 is measured. ⚠ Everything on the drums and the 808 is **collective-lineage inference from 808 Mafia**, marked `[LOW-CONF]` in the research and repeated here: the 808-kick-doubling-the-sub (`lockTo808` 1, `followRootsProb` 0.96), the 1/16 hat stream with triplet fills, the saturated sustained 808, and above all the **flat velocity** — `velocities.main` [0.82,0.9] against ghosts at [0.72,0.8] with `humanize.velocityVar` 0.04, which is the one parameter that would most obviously be wrong if a real interview ever surfaced. ⚠ The 2-bar-cell claim is `[LOW-CONF]` too; `twoBarCellRepeat` and the static-tonic families are built on it. ⚠ Boundary fact worth keeping: his origin is Chicago but his verified board is Atlanta-lineage trap, **not drill** — the one Chicago-artist credit is the only place the slower ~83 pole appears, and this model does not generate it. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
